#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Permanently delete archived Codex sessions.

Removes archived transcripts from <codex_home>/archived_sessions/ together with
their rows in state_5.sqlite and thread_history_1.sqlite and their lines in
history.jsonl and session_index.jsonl. Archived thread rows whose transcript is
already missing are removed too, as are transcript files in archived_sessions/
that no thread row references. Unarchived threads and unrelated data are left
alone.

Safety model
- Fails closed. Both databases must exist and match the pinned migration list
  and the exact schema constants below (tables, columns, constraints, indexes,
  triggers). Any mismatch aborts before anything is deleted. The constants are
  never updated automatically; re-pin them deliberately after inspecting a new
  Codex version.
- Every candidate is validated first. Rows whose rollout_path is not a rollout
  file directly inside archived_sessions/ named after the thread id, symlinks,
  and files that belong to unarchived threads are skipped and reported.
- Locks first, deletes second. flock() on both jsonl files and BEGIN IMMEDIATE
  on both databases are acquired up front. If any lock cannot be acquired
  within --lock-timeout the script exits without changing anything. Threads
  whose thread-writer-locks/<id>.lock is currently held are skipped.
- Write order: database deletes (uncommitted) -> jsonl rewrite under flock ->
  commit thread_history_1 -> commit state_5 -> unlink transcript files. A crash
  at any point leaves a state that the next run reclaims (rows without files
  and files without rows are both candidates).

Known limitations
- The two databases cannot share one transaction, so a crash between the two
  commits leaves thread_history rows deleted while the state row still exists.
  The next run removes the state row.
- jsonl files are rewritten in place (truncate + write) while holding flock so
  that a Codex process which opened the file before the rewrite still appends
  to the live inode. A crash inside that window can leave the file truncated.
  A Codex build that appends without taking flock could lose an append that
  lands inside the same window.
- Disk space inside the sqlite files is returned to the freelist, not to the
  filesystem; no VACUUM is run.
- Codex Desktop keeps its own sidebar catalog (sqlite/codex-dev.db and
  .codex-global-state.json). Those are not touched; the sidebar may keep
  showing deleted threads until the app rescans.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

